/-!
# Selection from nonzero support on the chosen minimal module

The original source hypotheses supply an actual minimal FundamentalSetup.
Nonzero moved support in a component-fixed subgroup gives crossed
centralization by minimality. The actual moving local Thompson case now
supplies that support from the complete B.5 two-factor classification. The moving local Thompson branch now has literal rank-one or binary-three
coordinates on every minimal full module.
The original source hypotheses now also select a minimal setup with the
complete B.5 two-factor structure on its actual full action. Its offender
subgroup is precisely the image of the original L₀. Literal natural
coordinates now imply full/local equality, including the fixed kernel.
The moving local Thompson branch now has either the literal rank-one
natural coordinates or an actual SL₃(2) component. The component reduction
uses all odd-prime rank bounds. Full movement now excludes the exceptional
four-space, and the SL₃(2) branch has literal natural or doubled coordinates
on its original moved subgroup and fixed kernel. Minimality and the actual
cubic residual now exclude the doubled row. The centralizing-local-Thompson branch now has a faithful irreducible full
action and a normally embedded product of the original component quotients.
The component models and both tensor exclusions now supply a nonzero
component-fixed vector and complete quiet support and point control.
The full/local Thompson comparison now covers Suzuki components as well.
Thus every source component supplies fixed-support rows and module choice,
and point selection retains only the named external normalizer input.
The separate even positive fixed-layer contract is also proved: direct
point control, the containing-component exclusions and the independent
generation bound construct the small offender excluded by the original
rank-one marking. Rank-one point control also holds when the two moved factors have
intersection of order at most two, even if their individual fixed kernels are
larger; the original uniqueness theorem controls the common intersection. Local and full Thompson centralization now agree when three divides
the component quotient order; full movement in those cases supplies the
literal natural rows and their point-selection application.

The point-control application uses the completed binary-three positive-
kernel calculation and the rank-one Baumann calculations. Its rank-one
fixed-layer input is discharged; source 10.2.5's half-Sylow normalizer bound
retains its independent producer.
The separate direct rank-one route now discharges point control for every
fixed kernel and field degree using original arbitrary-local-Sylow 1.2.8.
The direct consumers below require only fixed support and the independently
owned binary-three normalizer input; they do not require uniform rank-one
fixed-layer stabilization. The older conditional fixed-layer APIs are retained.
The quiet core-fusion family now supplies both alternatives of 3.2.10(6)
for the full module. Its literal 8.3.14 application retains the tensor
non-FF, point-core, hyperplane and numerical source obligations.
The marked tensor-radical calculation now discharges that hyperplane premise
from literal equivariant coordinates on S = O₂(XT), retaining the same point.
The concrete binary tensor Sylow action is now proved non-FF by quadratic
replacement and checked displacement bounds. Coordinates on the original
Sylow automizer transport this result to the actual local action, removing
the explicit non-FF premise from its fusion and weak-closure applications.
A marking of the original T-action in the full tensor group now constructs
these Sylow-automizer coordinates, allowing the original centralizer kernel.
A single surjective marking of the original L₀T action now also constructs
the radical coordinates on the literal S = O₂(XT), preserving the same point.
The residual/Sylow identity identifies XT with C_{L₀T}(z), and the paired
point-parabolic two-core supplies the radical image. Recognizing that original
tensor action, the point-core inclusion and the strict r bound remain source
obligations.
The concrete wreath actor is now identified with the tensor action group.
Recognition of the original linear image constructs its surjective marking,
and Sylow conjugacy marks every prescribed nonidentity T-fixed point as the
pure tensor. The action kernel is proved to be the literal C_{L₀T}(V).
Thus a separate point marking is no longer a premise of the weak-closure
application. The full tensor linear normalizer is now proved to be the
wreath group. Coordinates on L₀ alone therefore determine the full L₀T
image: the original exchanger cannot lie in the base image modulo its
two-group action kernel. Tensor-base irreducibility now identifies the original seed, local module
and full module. The same coordinates identify the full ambient automizer
and the exact two-core kernel. Its minimum involution displacement is three;
the proved E.6.3 theorem therefore gives r ≥ 3 and s = 3 on the original
module. The literal weak-closure application uses these source equalities
without a separate cubic-divisibility premise. Base-module recognition,
the strictly stronger r > 3, point-core inclusion and quiet tensor exclusions
remain open.
The outer-involution calculation now places every elementary (F−1)-offender
in the image of the original component orbit, through the same full automizer
coordinates. This supplies H.4.10(3) in the strict-r argument; the preceding
ambient centralizer analysis in 8.3.7--10 is still required.
The initial ambient bounds of 8.3.5 and the generation-one containment of
8.3.6 now follow from the literal tensor-radical hyperplanes and s=3.
The weak closures W₀(S,V) and W₁(S,V) are computed in Q=O₂(L₀T).
The generation-two endpoint also consumes the proved W₂(S,V) bound;
the point-core, strict-r and escaping-overgroup inputs remain with the
ambient centralizer argument, along with tensor-base recognition.
The original point parabolic now has its wreath quotient, and its literal
two-residual lies in Ξ(G,T). Chapter1 embedding applies to every cubic Sylow
subgroup, retaining the adequate Held alternative. The original S is Sylow
in the section centralizer and has normalizer contained in M; its solvable
section overgroups are controlled. The original cubic automizer is now
identified with D₈, excluding the cyclic symplectic embedding alternative. The
Suzuki components of the literal section centralizer now lie in M, even
when T exchanges components. The parameter-one case uses normal-section
weak closure; the larger-parameter case uses actual Hall faithfulness and
three-freeness, without a generation equality through the two-core.
The full section centralizer now lies in M: its components are analyzed in
N_{G_z}(X), source component generation controls its residual, and a solvable
supplement containing the same S is controlled by 8.3.6. The relative Frattini
product gives N_{G_z}(X) ≤ M. Quiet point escape excludes normality of X in
G_z, so the actual K_z strictly contains X and is a detected C-component
orbit, with its original cubic commutator and quotient markings retained.
The original full factorization and larger binary-field exclusions are
proved. In the remaining Held branch, every three-free T-invariant subgroup
of G_z lies in M, with the full quotient-action kernel retained. A faithful
elementary-nine action on a T-invariant fixed space containing z supplies
the corresponding ambient centralizer bound. The original plane-radical
fixed space is now constructed and lies in C₁(T,V), giving
C_G(C₁(T,V)) ≤ M in the Held branch. The zeroth weak closure now
centralizes V: fixed-space rigidity forces every conjugate into the paired
plane radicals, and two tensor vectors detect their action. Source uniqueness
controls its normalizer. The literal rank-one E-generation argument therefore
proves n(H)=2 for every original H-star in the retained Held branch, as in
8.3.9. The ambient Held exclusion is now proved using the actual K_z T
and its generation-one local pair. Binary flag geometry supplies the same
three-free centralizer bound in the invariant L₄(2) and L₅(2) rows; their
original joins also have generation one. E.3.19 excludes all three invariant
rows, leaving only exchanged odd-prime PSL₂ components in the literal
factorization. The exchanged odd-prime argument now controls every
three-free T-invariant subgroup of G_z: its two-residual has odd image on
the marked PSL₂ square, and the full action kernel has Sylow intersection
inside the original S. Minimal H-star descent and normal-section E-generation
then give the ambient containment. Thus the original plane-fixed centralizer
and C₁(T,V) centralizer lie in M in every model, and every original H-star
has generation parameter two. The proved E-generation of the actual KT
for primes at least seven excludes those primes; the order-three quotient
fails perfection. Thus the surviving original component quotient is PSL₂(5),
with the full factorization and cubic marking preserved. The same actual
component now supplies both PSL₂(5) and PSL₂(4) markings and the original
two-conjugate product. The full Borel is X(T intersect K_z)=K_z intersect M;
K_z T is an H-star of generation two and K_z T intersect M=XT. The full
section-action kernel is now proved to be a two-group, yielding G_z=K_zT,
M_z=XT, the two-group bound on C_M(V), and M=L₀T. The actual odd-order
tensor displacement and six-space geometry now give r(G,V)>3 via E.6.27,
discharging 8.3.11 in the original weak-closure consumer.
The original five-space now has both irreducible layers and rank five.
G.2.2 puts its literal K_z-closure in the point two-core and proves its
reduced central image modulo the original point. If the whole module is not
in that core, its intersection with the core is exactly this five-space.
The contrary branch now constructs the literal C_G(z)/O₂(C_G(z)) action on
U/⟨z⟩ and its quadratic dual offender. The original V-image and V₅/⟨z⟩
both have order sixteen, and the V-image is normal in the original Borel
image. Its actual normal-square quotient now identifies that offender as a
Sylow subgroup of K_z modulo the full point core; two conjugates generate
that image, with the conjugator lifted to the original K_z. The closure is
now the join of the two corresponding five-spaces. Their common fixed line
gives dimension at most eight on the literal U/⟨z⟩, and the same actor is
an offender on that module as well as on its dual. The original offender
now survives faithfully with order sixteen. The faithful image of this
same section has trivial two-core, and its nontrivial offender subgroup
satisfies the complete adequate B.5 alternatives. The full action kernel
and both individual-cubic fixed-point layers remain explicit. Upper irreducibility
now supplies best-offender generation of the literal square, whose whole
action is faithful. The B.5 comparison constructs two natural SL₂(4) summands
in the original eight-space and proves that its fixed kernel vanishes.
The individual diagonal cubic is now selected in the original cubic plane
and lifted through the full point two-core. It is fixed-point-free on both
literal layers, completing point-core containment and its 8.3.14 weak-closure
application. When the original seed is the full module, the binary-three
quotient action now constructs actual tensor coordinates and the literal base
image. The Steinberg alternative is excluded using the full ambient cubic
bound. The tensor contradiction therefore forces a proper seed in this quiet
binary-three branch. The proper-seed support and quiet rank-one exclusion
complete the fixed-support rows and module choice. The separate even positive
fixed-layer contract is proved in RankOne.EvenPositive and is applied below.
Selection retains the named stabilizer-normalizer premise. That bound remains with
qt-d-10-nona5-half-sylow-unique, and qt-d-10-nona5-point-control retains
the final discharge of the point-component exclusion inputs.

Source: Aschbacher--Smith, 3.2.6, 10.1.1--2 and 10.2.3,8--12,
printed pp.581,741--742,745--755.
-/

@[expose] public section
open Subgroup Theory.GroupTheory Stellmacher
namespace CFSG.Quasithin.Chapter10.PointControl.Selection.ModuleChoice
open Chapter3.Section02
variable {G : Type*} [Group G] [Finite G] {T : Sylow 2 G}

/-- The outstanding selected-module classification, expressed before crossed
centralization. Its support must lie in the original full module. -/
def FixedSupportRowsAt (T : Sylow 2 G) : Prop :=
  1 < (maximalTwoLocalOvergroups (T : Subgroup G)).ncard →
  ∀ (L : Subgroup G) (D : FundamentalSetup KnownSimple.IsKnownSimple T L),
    D.IsMinimalFullModule →
    ¬ (T : Subgroup G) ≤ normalizer (L : Set G) →
    ¬ Nonempty ((L ⧸ pCore 2 L) ≃* alternatingGroup (Fin 5)) →
    ∃ t ∈ (T : Subgroup G), t ∉ normalizer (L : Set G) ∧
      ⁅D.fullModule ⊓ centralizer
        (L.map (MulAut.conj t).toMonoidHom : Set G),L⁆ ≠ ⊥ ∧
      (elementaryAbelianMaxJ (T : Subgroup G) ≤ centralizer (D.fullModule : Set G) ∨
        (D.fullModule = D.moduleSubgroup ∧ (RankOneNatural D ∨ BinaryThreeNatural D)))

/-- Minimal source selection and the nonzero support argument discharge the
choice and crossed-centralization clauses of the literal module consumer. -/
theorem moduleChoiceAt_of_fixed_support_rows
    (hG : IsSimpleQTKE KnownSimple.IsKnownSimple G)
    (hrows : FixedSupportRowsAt T) : ModuleChoiceAt T := by
  intro hmany L hL hf hq hn hna
  obtain ⟨D,hmin⟩ := FundamentalSetup.exists_minimal_fullModule_of_source hG hL hf hq
  obtain ⟨t,ht,htn,hsupport,hmodels⟩ := hrows hmany L D hmin hn hna
  exact ⟨D,hmin,t,ht,htn,crossed_of_minimal_fixed_support D hmin ht htn hsupport,hmodels⟩

/-- Apply nonzero support to the exact point-Sylow selection contract.
The independent half-Sylow uniqueness remains inside its conclusion. -/
theorem selectionAt_of_fixed_support_rows
    (hG : IsSimpleQTKE KnownSimple.IsKnownSimple G)
    (hrows : FixedSupportRowsAt T) (hrank : RankOne.FixedLayerAt T)
    (hN : HalfUniqueness.StabilizerNormalizerAt T) : SelectionAt T :=
  BinaryThree.selectionAt_of_module_choice_rank_one_and_stabilizer
    (moduleChoiceAt_of_fixed_support_rows hG hrows)
    (RankOne.rankOneGeometryAt_of_fixed_layer hrank) hN

/-- Actual application to the parent's point-centralizer consumer. Each
remaining source input keeps its original owner and original point maps. -/
theorem source_nonnormalPointControl_of_fixed_support_rows
    (hG : IsSimpleQTKE KnownSimple.IsKnownSimple G)
    (hrows : FixedSupportRowsAt T) (hrank : RankOne.FixedLayerAt T)
    (hN : HalfUniqueness.StabilizerNormalizerAt T) (hhalf : HalfSylowUniqueAt T)
    (hquasi : QuasisimpleExclusionAt T) (hconstrained : ConstrainedExclusionAt T) :
    SourceNonnormalPointControlAt T :=
  source_nonnormalPointControl_of_sourceSteps hhalf
    (selectionAt_of_fixed_support_rows hG hrows hrank hN) hquasi hconstrained

/-- Odd-degree and zero-kernel rank-one geometry is discharged; only even
positive kernels remain alongside selected-module classification. -/
theorem selectionAt_of_even_positive_fixed_support_rows
    (hG : IsSimpleQTKE KnownSimple.IsKnownSimple G)
    (hrows : FixedSupportRowsAt T) (heven : RankOne.EvenPositiveFixedLayerAt T)
    (hN : HalfUniqueness.StabilizerNormalizerAt T) : SelectionAt T :=
  selectionAt_of_fixed_support_rows hG hrows
    (RankOne.fixedLayerAt_of_even_positive heven) hN

/-- The parent point-centralizer consumer with every odd-degree rank-one
fixed-layer premise discharged on the original point and module. -/
theorem source_nonnormalPointControl_of_even_positive_fixed_support_rows
    (hG : IsSimpleQTKE KnownSimple.IsKnownSimple G)
    (hrows : FixedSupportRowsAt T) (heven : RankOne.EvenPositiveFixedLayerAt T)
    (hN : HalfUniqueness.StabilizerNormalizerAt T) (hhalf : HalfSylowUniqueAt T)
    (hquasi : QuasisimpleExclusionAt T) (hconstrained : ConstrainedExclusionAt T) :
    SourceNonnormalPointControlAt T :=
  source_nonnormalPointControl_of_sourceSteps hhalf
    (selectionAt_of_even_positive_fixed_support_rows hG hrows heven hN) hquasi hconstrained

/-- The residual classification in the moving-Thompson branch: full/local
identity and literal natural coordinates. Crossed support is now derived. -/
def MovingModelsAt (T : Sylow 2 G) : Prop :=
  1 < (maximalTwoLocalOvergroups (T : Subgroup G)).ncard →
  ∀ (L : Subgroup G) (D : FundamentalSetup KnownSimple.IsKnownSimple T L),
    D.IsMinimalFullModule →
    ¬ (T : Subgroup G) ≤ normalizer (L : Set G) →
    ¬ Nonempty ((L ⧸ pCore 2 L) ≃* alternatingGroup (Fin 5)) →
    ¬ elementaryAbelianMaxJ (T : Subgroup G) ≤ centralizer (D.fullModule : Set G) →
    D.fullModule = D.moduleSubgroup ∧ (RankOneNatural D ∨ BinaryThreeNatural D)

/-- The remaining moving-row recognition on the literal central quotient.
Full/local equality is a consequence of these coordinates. -/
def MovingNaturalRowsAt (T : Sylow 2 G) : Prop :=
  1 < (maximalTwoLocalOvergroups (T : Subgroup G)).ncard →
  ∀ (L : Subgroup G) (D : FundamentalSetup KnownSimple.IsKnownSimple T L),
    D.IsMinimalFullModule →
    ¬ (T : Subgroup G) ≤ normalizer (L : Set G) →
    ¬ Nonempty ((L ⧸ pCore 2 L) ≃* alternatingGroup (Fin 5)) →
    ¬ elementaryAbelianMaxJ (T : Subgroup G) ≤ centralizer (D.fullModule : Set G) →
    RankOneNatural D ∨ BinaryThreeNatural D

/-- The literal natural rows discharge the old full/local equality premise
on the same setup, without a new seed or a zero-kernel assumption. -/
theorem movingModelsAt_of_moving_natural_rows
    (hrows : MovingNaturalRowsAt T) : MovingModelsAt T := by
  intro hmany L D hmin hn hna hJ
  rcases hrows hmany L D hmin hn hna hJ with hrank | hthree
  · exact ⟨full_eq_local_of_rankOneNatural D hn hrank, Or.inl hrank⟩
  · exact ⟨full_eq_local_of_binaryThreeNatural D hn hthree, Or.inr hthree⟩

/-- The remaining support branch when J(T) centralizes the full module.
The non-FF tensor exclusions, not the B.5 moving-module theorem, supply it. -/
def QuietSupportAt (T : Sylow 2 G) : Prop :=
  1 < (maximalTwoLocalOvergroups (T : Subgroup G)).ncard →
  ∀ (L : Subgroup G) (D : FundamentalSetup KnownSimple.IsKnownSimple T L),
    D.IsMinimalFullModule →
    ¬ (T : Subgroup G) ≤ normalizer (L : Set G) →
    ¬ Nonempty ((L ⧸ pCore 2 L) ≃* alternatingGroup (Fin 5)) →
    elementaryAbelianMaxJ (T : Subgroup G) ≤ centralizer (D.fullModule : Set G) →
    ∃ t ∈ (T : Subgroup G), t ∉ normalizer (L : Set G) ∧
      ⁅D.fullModule ⊓ centralizer
        (L.map (MulAut.conj t).toMonoidHom : Set G),L⁆ ≠ ⊥

/-- The moving rows need no separate support hypothesis. The local B.5
classification and minimality supply it on the original full module. -/
theorem fixedSupportRowsAt_of_quiet_support_and_moving_models
    (hquiet : QuietSupportAt T) (hmodels : MovingModelsAt T) : FixedSupportRowsAt T := by
  intro hmany L D hmin hn hna
  by_cases hJ : elementaryAbelianMaxJ (T : Subgroup G) ≤ centralizer (D.fullModule : Set G)
  · obtain ⟨t,ht,htn,hs⟩ := hquiet hmany L D hmin hn hna hJ
    exact ⟨t,ht,htn,hs,Or.inl hJ⟩
  obtain ⟨hfull,hm⟩ := hmodels hmany L D hmin hn hna hJ
  obtain ⟨t,ht,htn,_,_⟩ := source_nonnormal_pair D.simpleQTKE D.maximal.1 hn
  have hlocal : ¬ elementaryAbelianMaxJ (T : Subgroup G) ≤
      centralizer (D.moduleSubgroup : Set G) := hfull ▸ hJ
  exact ⟨t,ht,htn,fixed_support_moved_of_crossed D ht htn
    (crossed_of_local_thompson D hlocal ht htn), Or.inr ⟨hfull,hm⟩⟩

/-- Actual selection application with the moving-row support premise removed.
The remaining source rows and even fixed-layer stabilization stay with this
owner; the half-Sylow normalizer stays with its original external owner. -/
